<article class="ltx_document">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Wanying Ren
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>East China Normal University, Shanghai, China
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Xin Song
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>East China Normal University, Shanghai, China
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Futing Wang
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Westlake University, Hangzhou, China
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Guoxiu He
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>East China Normal University, Shanghai, China
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Correspondence to: </span><a href="mailto:gxhe@fem.ecnu.edu.cn">gxhe@fem.ecnu.edu.cn</a>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Aixin Sun
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Nanyang Technological University, Singapore
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Parameter-based knowledge editing updates the internal knowledge of large language models (LLMs) via localized weight modifications and has attracted significant attention.
However, most existing methods overlook fundamental theoretical limitations and are rarely evaluated under realistic, practice-oriented settings.
In this paper, we first present a theoretical analysis based on the dimensional Collapse Hypothesis, explaining how localized parameter edits can propagate along fragile directions in the representation space, inducing global interference and ultimately causing reasoning collapse.
Building on this insight, we conduct a comprehensive empirical evaluation by systematically varying knowledge complexity, number of edits, evaluation dimensions, and baseline methods.
Our results show that parameter-based editing methods consistently damage core LLM capabilities.
In contrast, a simple retrieval-based baseline achieves consistently stronger performance than all parameter-editing methods across all evaluated conditions.
These findings highlight that preserving the fundamental capabilities of LLMs after knowledge editing should be a central concern for future research.</p>
  
</div><span id="id1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_affiliationnotice"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">affiliationnotice: </span>Equal contribution</span></span></span>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large language models (LLMs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib114" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zeng et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib87" title="" class="ltx_ref">Touvron et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2023</a>)</cite> acquire extensive world knowledge <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">AlKhamissi et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib122" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2023c</a>)</cite> and remarkable contextual reasoning abilities <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al., </a>)</cite> through large-scale pre-training and post-training <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brown et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. However, as world knowledge continuously evolves, some information encoded in LLMs inevitably becomes outdated or inaccurate <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mousavi et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ji et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. To address this issue, knowledge editing methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib85" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sinitsin et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib80" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rawat et al., 2021</a>)</cite> have been proposed to incorporate newly acquired knowledge into LLMs through minimal parameter modifications or external knowledge storage.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2606.00570v1/fig_model-intro-v3.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:448/258;" width="448" height="258" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S1.F1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Training and inference workflows for four types of knowledge editing methods. The training process is shown above the <span id="S1.F1.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#008080;">green line</span>, while the inference stage is shown below. (a) and (b) perform knowledge editing by modifying the LLM parameters.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Knowledge editing methods can be broadly categorized into four main types, distinguished by how they adjust LLM parameters to incorporate collected textual knowledge. Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> illustrates their respective workflows during training and inference. (a) <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Locate-then-edit</span> methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meng et al., 2022b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meng et al., </a>; <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024c</a>)</cite> assume that specific knowledge is associated with certain LLM parameters; they first <span id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">locate</span> the neurons corresponding to the target knowledge and then <span id="S1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">edit</span> the relevant parameters to incorporate the new knowledge. (b) <span id="S1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Meta-learning</span>-based methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">De Cao et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib86" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tan et al., </a>)</cite> assume that the patterns of parameter changes during knowledge updates can be learned; an editor model is trained to modify the parameters in specific layers of the LLM associated with the target knowledge. (c) <span id="S1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Additional parameter</span>-based methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib113" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib91" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024b</a>)</cite> assume that new knowledge can be stored in additional parameters external to the LLM; adapter layers or other auxiliary components are introduced to encode new knowledge while leaving the base LLM parameters unchanged. (d) <span id="S1.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">External memory</span>-based methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hartvigsen et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mitchell et al., 2022</a>)</cite> store updated knowledge as texts, embeddings, hidden states, or lightweight models; during inference, the most relevant information is retrieved to support LLM reasoning without modifying LLM parameters.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Methods that directly modify the internal parameters of LLMs have attracted substantial research attention, partly because they appear more principled, incur no additional inference-time overhead compared to the original model, and often achieve seemingly perfect knowledge updates under specific evaluation protocols. <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">From a theoretical perspective</span>, however, we argue that these advantages can be misleading. Although localized parameter edits may be effective for isolated factual updates, dimensional collapse in LLM representation spaces creates fragile low-variance directions where even small weight perturbations are disproportionately amplified. This amplification distorts representation geometry and propagates through subsequent layers, leading to catastrophic forgetting of unrelated knowledge, degraded generalization, and impaired reasoning. In this work, we develop a theoretical analysis in Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> to characterize the fundamental limitations of parameter-based knowledge editing methods.
<span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">From an empirical perspective</span>, most existing studies have not fully evaluated knowledge editing methods in terms of LLMs’ core capabilities and realistic application scenarios. While some work has highlighted deficiencies in specific evaluation dimensions, these discussions remain fragmented and have yet to form a widely accepted, systematic framework. LLMs encode not only isolated factual knowledge (<span id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">e.g.</span>, triples) but also complex event-level knowledge, and their capabilities extend beyond answering simple facts to flexible knowledge application and deeper reasoning, such as reverse relation inference and multi-hop reasoning. Realistic editing scenarios should involve sequential, continuous updates rather than single-shot edits, and parameter-based editing methods should be systematically compared with external-knowledge-based paradigms (<span id="S1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">e.g.</span>, retrieval-augmented generation (RAG)). Empirical results are shown in Section <a href="#S5" title="5 Empirical Study ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Our contributions are threefold <span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
            
            
            
            
            
            
            
          The code is available at <a href="https://github.com/ECNU-Text-Computing/Revisiting-Parameter-Based-Knowledge-Editing" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">GitHub Link</a>.</span></span></span>:</p>
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We develop a geometric theoretical framework that characterizes how localized parameter edits destabilize LLM representations.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We perform a comprehensive empirical evaluation under realistic settings, showing that such edits consistently degrade core LLM capabilities.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our results clarify the fundamental limitations of parameter-based knowledge editing and highlight the stability–efficiency trade-off in the mechanisms.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Knowledge editing in LLMs has received increasing attention, with numerous methods aimed at improving accuracy and efficiency
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li &amp; Chu, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fang et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib82" title="" class="ltx_ref">Scialanga et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nie et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib123" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., </a>)</cite>. While most work focuses on improving factual correctness for isolated updates, a few studies have also highlighted potential limitations of parameter-based editing.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For example, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib79" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pinter &amp; Elhadad (2023)</a></cite> noted conflicts between optimizing factual consistency and pre-training objectives, while <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib90" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2025)</a></cite> quantified the <span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">ripple effect</span> of local edits on hidden representations, leading to degraded model performance. Similarly, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gu et al. (2024b)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib103" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xie et al. (2025)</a></cite> observed that improving factual accuracy often undermines reasoning, natural language inference, and question answering,
with edits to as little as 1% of parameters harming unrelated tasks.
Furthermore, minor parameter modifications can reduce text coherence <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib109" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2024d</a>)</cite>, trigger model collapse in some specific methods such as ROME <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib110" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2024e</a>)</cite>, amplify existing biases <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Halevy et al., 2024</a>)</cite> or yield misleading evaluations under teacher-forcing settings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib111" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Mechanistic analyses have highlighted failure modes in locate-and-edit methods, offering theoretical insights into key-value representations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib105" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yan et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. A broader discussion appears in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS1" title="A.1 Knowledge Editing Methods ‣ Appendix A Related Work ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">While these studies have significantly enhanced empirical understanding of knowledge editing risks, their contributions remain largely phenomenological and scoped to specific phenomena: individual failure patterns, method variants, or isolated evaluation settings.
They have not yet converged into a unified theoretical framework that explains why parameter edits can broadly compromise LLM performance. Nor have they established comprehensive and consistent evaluation standards (as summarized in Table <a href="#A1.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ A.2 Summary of Experimental Settings ‣ Appendix A Related Work ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS2" title="A.2 Summary of Experimental Settings ‣ Appendix A Related Work ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2</span></a>) that capture core LLM capabilities and realistic application scenarios.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast, this paper makes two complementary contributions, balancing theoretical analysis and empirical evaluation.
First, we develop a mathematically grounded framework based on the dimensional Collapse Hypothesis, formally demonstrate that even minor parameter edits disrupt pre-training-induced knowledge geometry, resulting in systemic reasoning failures.
Second,
We conduct a comprehensive benchmark comparing parameter-based and external-knowledge-based paradigms
(<span id="S2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">e.g.</span>, RAG)
across diverse knowledge formats, reasoning-oriented LLMs, multi-step editing scenarios, and practical inference tasks. This evaluation incorporates extensive metrics for robustness and capability preservation, surpassing prior benchmarks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
Together, these contributions establish both theoretical underpinnings and empirical evidence for the limitations of parameter-based knowledge editing, uncovering potential oversights in critical application-relevant characteristics and
providing practical implications and actionable directions for future research.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Triplet-based knowledge editing focuses on updating a factual triple <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e=(h,r,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e=(h,r,t)</annotation></semantics></math> to a new triple <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{\prime}=(h,r,t_{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{\prime}=(h,r,t_{*})</annotation></semantics></math> within an original language model <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. For example, editing <span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(Barack Obama, born in, Hawaii)</span> to <span id="S3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(Barack Obama, born in, Kenya)</span>. The updated model <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is obtained by applying the editor <math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> to the initial model <math id="S3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and the knowledge update <math id="S3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>e</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{1}=\mathcal{E}(f^{0},e^{\prime}),\quad\text{such that}\ e^{\prime}\in f^{1}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>such that</mtext><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{1}=\mathcal{E}(f^{0},e^{\prime}),\quad\text{such that}\ e^{\prime}\in f^{1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Factual knowledge, such as triples, can be represented as a single prompt–answer pair <math id="S3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e=[(x,y)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e=[(x,y)]</annotation></semantics></math>, whereas more complex event is represented using multiple prompt–answer pairs <math id="S3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e=[(x_{1},y_{1}),\cdots,(x_{m},y_{m})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e=[(x_{1},y_{1}),\cdots,(x_{m},y_{m})]</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the prompt to elicit the knowledge, <math id="S3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> represents the answer, and <math id="S3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the number of pairs.
After editing, the updated model <math id="S3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> should
outputs the ground truth <math id="S3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> for the corresponding prompts <math id="S3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Editing Scenarios.</span> <span id="S3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Single Editing</span> refers to the process where the LLM updates only one piece of knowledge (<math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E=[e_{1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E=[e_{1}]</annotation></semantics></math>) at a time.
<span id="S3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sequential Editing</span> also known as lifelong editing or continual editing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hartvigsen et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib113" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib91" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>, refers to repeatedly applying the editing method to update a sequence of knowledge <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{t}=[e_{1},e_{2},\ldots,e_{t}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{t}=[e_{1},e_{2},\ldots,e_{t}]</annotation></semantics></math>.
Consequently, the LLM evolves from <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> over multiple updates. Note that, in the sequential editing setting, each method operates differently: <span id="S3.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">locate-then-edit</span> methods modify a subset of the LLM’s parameters with each update; <span id="S3.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">meta-learning</span>-based methods use a learned editor to update the parameters of the target model;
<span id="S3.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">additional parameter</span>-based methods repeatedly update external parameter components; <span id="S3.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">external memory</span>-based methods only update an external knowledge base, such as a text corpus or vector store, that stores newly introduced knowledge.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Evaluation Dimensions.</span>
After <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> updates, the edited model <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is evaluated across four dimensions
 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib116" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="~~\bullet" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">~~\bullet</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reliability:</span>
The edited LLM should reliably produce the updated target outputs for the prompts in <math id="S3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, ensuring the persistence of the edits:</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{(x_{i},y_{i})\sim E_{t}}\mathbb{I}\{\arg\max_{y}f^{t}(y\mid x_{i})=y_{i}\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mi>𝕀</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>arg</mi><munder><mi>max</mi><mi>y</mi></munder><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{(x_{i},y_{i})\sim E_{t}}\mathbb{I}\{\arg\max_{y}f^{t}(y\mid x_{i})=y_{i}\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="~~\bullet" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">~~\bullet</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Generalization:</span> The edited LLM should extend beyond the exact edits and correctly respond to paraphrased prompts, denoted as <math id="S3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N(E_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N(E_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{(x_{i},y_{i})\sim N(E_{t})}\mathbb{I}\{\arg\max_{y}f^{t}(y\mid x_{i})=y_{i}\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mi>𝕀</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>arg</mi><munder><mi>max</mi><mi>y</mi></munder><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{(x_{i},y_{i})\sim N(E_{t})}\mathbb{I}\{\arg\max_{y}f^{t}(y\mid x_{i})=y_{i}\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="~~\bullet" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">~~\bullet</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Locality:</span>
The edited LLM should retain its original behavior for queries unrelated to the edits, denoted <math id="S3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(E_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(E_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, ensuring stability and minimal unintended changes:</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{(x_{i},y_{i})\sim O(E_{t})}\mathbb{I}\{f^{t}(y\mid x_{i})=f^{0}(y\mid x_{i})\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mi>𝕀</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{(x_{i},y_{i})\sim O(E_{t})}\mathbb{I}\{f^{t}(y\mid x_{i})=f^{0}(y\mid x_{i})\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="~~\bullet" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">~~\bullet</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Portability:</span>
The edited LLM should propagate the edits effectively, enabling downstream reasoning <math id="S3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(E_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(E_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, including handling aliases, causal relationships, and reverse relations. Despite its importance, this dimension has been overlooked in many studies (shown in Table <a href="#A1.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ A.2 Summary of Experimental Settings ‣ Appendix A Related Work ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>):</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{(x_{i},y_{i})\sim D(E_{t})}\mathbb{I}\{\arg\max_{y}f^{t}(y\mid x_{i})=y_{i}\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mi>𝕀</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>arg</mi><munder><mi>max</mi><mi>y</mi></munder><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{(x_{i},y_{i})\sim D(E_{t})}\mathbb{I}\{\arg\max_{y}f^{t}(y\mid x_{i})=y_{i}\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Evaluation for General Tasks.</span>
In addition to the knowledge editing evaluation dimensions, it is also important to assess whether the edited LLM retains its ability to handle general tasks.
In this study, mathematical reasoning datasets are utilized to evaluate the edited model’s reasoning capabilities. A successful editing process achieves precise targeted updates while preserving the LLM’s core capabilities, including
generalization, reasoning, and performance on unrelated knowledge.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Theoretical Analysis</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section develops a geometric framework for understanding why parameter-based knowledge editing can induce disproportionate side effects in large language models. Our analysis is motivated by a simple observation: if hidden representations are dimensionally collapsed, then some representation directions have extremely weak native signal scales, making them vulnerable to even localized parameter perturbations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We formalize this intuition in four steps. First, we introduce the setup and the working assumptions under which the mechanism is analyzable (Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Working Hypotheses and Problem Formulation ‣ 4 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>). Second, we define a relative-sensitivity metric and show why perturbations can be strongly amplified in low-singular-value directions (Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Relative Perturbation Amplification in Collapsed Directions ‣ 4 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>). Third, we discuss how such distortions accumulate under sequential editing and why practical behavior may become even worse once representation stability breaks down (Section <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 Cumulative Effects in Sequential Editing ‣ 4 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>).
Finally, we bridge the representation-level analysis to model performance by exploring how excessive distortions correlate with downstream capability degradation, providing empirical evidence for the practical significance of this geometric vulnerability (Section <a href="#S4.SS4" title="4.4 From Representation Distortion to Capability Degradation ‣ 4 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Our goal is not to fully characterize edited LLM dynamics but to isolate a minimal vulnerability: dimensional collapse makes representations fragile in low-variance directions, even in locally stable regimes.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Working Hypotheses and Problem Formulation</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We consider a feed-forward network (<math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="FFN" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">FFN</annotation></semantics></math>) layer in a Transformer model:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S4.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h=\phi(Wx)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h=\phi(Wx),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">where
<math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{in}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{in}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the input vector,
<math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d_{in}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d_{in}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the weight matrix,
<math id="S4.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi:\mathbb{R}^{d}\to\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi:\mathbb{R}^{d}\to\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is an elementwise nonlinearity, and
<math id="S4.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is the resulting hidden representation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">A parameter-editing operation modifies the layer locally by adding a perturbation <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangle W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">△</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangle W</annotation></semantics></math>, producing
<math id="S4.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{edited}=W+\triangle W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">△</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{edited}=W+\triangle W</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Given a set of <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> input samples, let
<math id="S4.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H=[h^{(1)},h^{(2)},\ldots,h^{(n)}]\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H=[h^{(1)},h^{(2)},\ldots,h^{(n)}]\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}</annotation></semantics></math>
denote the centered hidden representation matrix at this layer. We perform singular value decomposition: <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H=U\Sigma V^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H=U\Sigma V^{T}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U=[u_{1},u_{2},\ldots,u_{d}]\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U=[u_{1},u_{2},\ldots,u_{d}]\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> is an orthonormal basis of principal directions, and the singular values are ordered as
<math id="S4.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{1}\geq\sigma_{2}\geq\cdots\geq\sigma_{d}\geq 0." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">≥</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">≥</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{1}\geq\sigma_{2}\geq\cdots\geq\sigma_{d}\geq 0.</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Dimensional Collapse Hypothesis)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We assume that the hidden representation space of the pre-trained model exhibits dimensional collapse in a spectral sense: its singular-value spectrum is highly anisotropic, with a strongly compressed low-variance tail.
Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\min}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\min}</annotation></semantics></math> be the smallest non-zero singular value. When <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\min}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\min}</annotation></semantics></math> is small, the associated direction has a weak signal scale.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">This assumption is supported by empirical measurements, which show low effective rank, rapidly decaying singular values, and large condition numbers (e.g., Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct in Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.1 Working Hypotheses and Problem Formulation ‣ 4 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). A detailed evaluation of these characteristics is provided in Appendix <a href="#A2.SS1" title="B.1 Hypothesis : Dimensional Collapse ‣ Appendix B Justifications for Working Hypotheses and Empirical Support ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a> (Tables <a href="#A2.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ B.1 Hypothesis : Dimensional Collapse ‣ Appendix B Justifications for Working Hypotheses and Empirical Support ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>–<a href="#A2.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ B.1 Hypothesis : Dimensional Collapse ‣ Appendix B Justifications for Working Hypotheses and Empirical Support ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 1</span>: </span><span id="S4.T1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Empirical evidence of dimensional collapse in the hidden representations of Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct.
The hidden representation space exhibits clear characteristics of dimensional collapse: the effective rank <math id="S4.T1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> is significantly smaller than the feature dimension, the smallest singular value <math id="S4.T1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\min}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\min}</annotation></semantics></math> is extremely small (<math id="S4.T1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim 10^{-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim 10^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math>), and the condition number is very large (<math id="S4.T1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;10^{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>6</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;10^{6}</annotation></semantics></math>).
</span></figcaption>
<table id="S4.T1.5" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S4.T1.5.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T1.5.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt">layer</td>
<td id="S4.T1.5.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><math id="S4.T1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S4.T1.5.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><math id="S4.T1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\text{eff}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mtext>eff</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\text{eff}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S4.T1.5.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><math id="S4.T1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r/d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r/d</annotation></semantics></math> (%)</td>
<td id="S4.T1.5.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_tt"><math id="S4.T1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S4.T1.5.1.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><math id="S4.T1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\min}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\min}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S4.T1.5.1.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><math id="S4.T1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{cond}(\sigma_{1}/\sigma_{\min})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>cond</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{cond}(\sigma_{1}/\sigma_{\min})</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.5.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T1.5.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">5</td>
<td id="S4.T1.5.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">4096</td>
<td id="S4.T1.5.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">3249</td>
<td id="S4.T1.5.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">79.3</td>
<td id="S4.T1.5.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_t">194.1</td>
<td id="S4.T1.5.2.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S4.T1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.38\times 10^{-6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.38</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.38\times 10^{-6}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S4.T1.5.2.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S4.T1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5.74\times 10^{7}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5.74</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>7</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5.74\times 10^{7}</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
