<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Cumulative Effects in Sequential Editing</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now extend the analysis to sequential editing, where the model undergoes a series of updates:
<math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{(0)}\to W_{(1)}\to\dots\to W_{(T)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{(0)}\to W_{(1)}\to\dots\to W_{(T)}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the hidden representation after the <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-th edit. The total representation change after <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> edits relative to the original model satisfies the telescoping identity</p>
<table id="S4.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangle h^{(T)}=h^{(T)}-h^{(0)}=\sum_{t=1}^{T}\triangle h_{\text{instant}}^{(t)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">△</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">△</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mtext>instant</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangle h^{(T)}=h^{(T)}-h^{(0)}=\sum_{t=1}^{T}\triangle h_{\text{instant}}^{(t)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangle h_{\text{instant}}^{(t)}=h^{(t)}-h^{(t-1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">△</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mtext>instant</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangle h_{\text{instant}}^{(t)}=h^{(t)}-h^{(t-1)}</annotation></semantics></math> is the instantaneous change induced by the <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-th edit.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Under the local stability approximation of Hypothesis <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem3" title="Assumption 4.3 (Local Representation Stability Hypothesis). ‣ 4.1 Working Hypotheses and Problem Formulation ‣ 4 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>, we use the original principal basis as an approximate common coordinate system across edits. Projecting onto a principal direction <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{\min}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{\min}</annotation></semantics></math> associated with the smallest non-zero singular value <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\min}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\min}</annotation></semantics></math>, and defining <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{\min}^{(t)}\triangleq u_{\min}^{T}\Delta h_{\text{inst}}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>min</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>≜</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>min</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mtext>inst</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{\min}^{(t)}\triangleq u_{\min}^{T}\Delta h_{\text{inst}}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">we obtain</p>
<table id="S4.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{\min}^{(T)}=u_{\min}^{T}\Delta h^{(T)}=\sum_{t=1}^{T}c_{\min}^{(t)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>min</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>min</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>min</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{\min}^{(T)}=u_{\min}^{T}\Delta h^{(T)}=\sum_{t=1}^{T}c_{\min}^{(t)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In a worst-case scenario, where edits produce coherent accumulations of perturbations, we obtain:</p>
<table id="S4.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|c_{\min}^{(T)}|\approx T\cdot\bar{\varepsilon}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>min</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ε</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|c_{\min}^{(T)}|\approx T\cdot\bar{\varepsilon},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>ε</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> is the average per-edit perturbation magnitude along <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{\min}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{\min}</annotation></semantics></math>. Substituting into Eq. (<a href="#S4.E9" title="Equation 9 ‣ 4.2 Relative Perturbation Amplification in Collapsed Directions ‣ 4 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) yields</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S4.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\min}^{(T)}=\frac{\sqrt{n}\,|c_{\min}^{(T)}|}{\sigma_{\min}}\approx T\cdot\frac{\sqrt{n}\,\bar{\varepsilon}}{\sigma_{\min}}=T\cdot R_{\min}^{(1)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>min</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>min</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mfrac><mrow><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ε</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>min</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\min}^{(T)}=\frac{\sqrt{n}\,|c_{\min}^{(T)}|}{\sigma_{\min}}\approx T\cdot\frac{\sqrt{n}\,\bar{\varepsilon}}{\sigma_{\min}}=T\cdot R_{\min}^{(1)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Under this idealized coherent local regime, distortion in weak directions grows linearly with the number of edits.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p10.1" class="ltx_p">This linear law assumes stable principal directions across edits, but in practice, this approximation breaks down as edits accumulate.
Our empirical measurements show that principal directions, especially in the low-variance tail, drift rapidly under sequential editing across multiple models and architectures (Appendix <a href="#A2.SS3" title="B.3 Hypothesis : Local Representation Stability ‣ Appendix B Justifications for Working Hypotheses and Empirical Support ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.3</span></a>, Tables <a href="#A2.T13" title="Table 13 ‣ B.3 Hypothesis : Local Representation Stability ‣ Appendix B Justifications for Working Hypotheses and Empirical Support ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>–<a href="#A2.T20" title="Table 20 ‣ B.3 Hypothesis : Local Representation Stability ‣ Appendix B Justifications for Working Hypotheses and Empirical Support ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a>). Therefore, practical sequential editing does not merely accumulate perturbations in a fixed vulnerable basis; it can also reorganize the basis itself, inducing broader representational instability.
This distinction strengthens, the main message of the theory. The fixed-basis analysis shows that cumulative fragility already arises even in a favorable local regime. Once basis drift and representation shattering are taken into account, practical degradation can be even more severe than this idealized prediction suggests.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Taken together, Sections <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Relative Perturbation Amplification in Collapsed Directions ‣ 4 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> and <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 Cumulative Effects in Sequential Editing ‣ 4 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a> suggest the following picture: dimensional collapse creates directions with extremely small native signal scales; small local edits are therefore disproportionately amplified in relative terms; and repeated editing compounds this vulnerability, eventually destabilizing the broader representation geometry.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>From Representation Distortion to Capability Degradation</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The analysis above is a representation-level statement.
Large <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> does not directly imply behavioral failure but signifies structural risk. When <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is large, the post-edit representation in <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> exceeds its native pre-edit scale.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To connect this to behavior, note that downstream computations depend not only on absolute activation magnitudes, but also on representation geometry, including relative positioning, subspace structure, and routing through subsequent nonlinear layers. Large relative distortions can therefore alter intermediate geometry in ways that propagate forward and potentially affect task behavior.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This interpretation is further supported by the correlation analysis between step-level <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-based statistics and sequential editing performance.
Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.2 Relative Perturbation Amplification in Collapsed Directions ‣ 4 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> reports the main Spearman correlation results, showing that larger amplification is generally associated with worse reliability, locality, and portability for several parameter-based editing methods.
Full experimental details and complete results across summary and aggregation choices are provided in Appendix <a href="#A2.SS6" title="B.6 Functional Relevance: Correlation with Editing Performance ‣ Appendix B Justifications for Working Hypotheses and Empirical Support ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.6</span></a>.
(Table <a href="#A2.T25" title="Table 25 ‣ B.6 Functional Relevance: Correlation with Editing Performance ‣ Appendix B Justifications for Working Hypotheses and Empirical Support ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">25</span></a>–<a href="#A2.T28" title="Table 28 ‣ B.6 Functional Relevance: Correlation with Editing Performance ‣ Appendix B Justifications for Working Hypotheses and Empirical Support ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">28</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Empirical Study</h2>

<figure id="S5.F3" class="ltx_figure">
<p id="S5.F3.3" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center ltx_align_center"><span id="S5.F3.3.1" class="ltx_text"><img src="2606.00570v1/fig_scale_llama3_revision.png" id="S5.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/143;" width="476" height="143" alt="Refer to caption"></span></p>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S5.F3.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
Performance changes of knowledge editing methods during sequential editing of Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct on the ZsRE dataset. The x-axis represents the number of edits: 1, 10, 100, and ‘All’ for the full dataset.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Experimental Settings</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate on Llama-2-7B-Chat <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib87" title="" class="ltx_ref">Touvron et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meta AI, 2024</a>)</cite>, Mistral-7B-Instruct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, and DeepSeek-R1-Distill-LLaMA-8B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, with larger-scale verification on Llama-2-13B and Qwen3-14B. The studied parameter-modifying methods span locate-then-edit (ROME <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meng et al., 2022b</a>)</cite>, MEMIT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meng et al., </a>)</cite>, PMET <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024c</a>)</cite>, RECT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gu et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>, AlphaEdit <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fang et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>, FT-L), meta-learning (MEND <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">De Cao et al., 2021</a>)</cite>), and additional-parameter methods (AdaLoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib118" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2023b</a>)</cite>, WISE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib91" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>); external memory baselines include GRACE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hartvigsen et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and SCR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
We use the ZsRE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Levy et al., 2017</a>)</cite>, WikiData<math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}_{\text{counterfact}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>counterfact</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\text{counterfact}}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cohen et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, and ELKEN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib77" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peng et al., 2024</a>)</cite> datasets for editing, and mathematical reasoning datasets for general tasks.
Evaluation adopts autoregressive decoding with semantic consistency judged by Qwen2.5-72B-Instruct across four dimensions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib116" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>.
A token-level locality check confirms the semantic results (Appendix <a href="#A3.SS5" title="C.5 Token-Level Locality Evaluation ‣ Appendix C Experiments and Results ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.5</span></a>). Full implementation details, hardware specifications, and metric calculation procedures are provided in Appendix <a href="#A3.SS1" title="C.1 Full Experimental Settings for the Benchmark Study ‣ Appendix C Experiments and Results ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Experimental Results</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ1: How do knowledge editing methods perform in practice-oriented settings?</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S5.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 5 Empirical Study ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> illustrates the intermediate performance of Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct on the ZsRE dataset as the number of edits increases from 1 to 10, 100, and finally to the full dataset. For the corresponding results of Llama-2-7B-Chat and Mistral-7B-Instruct, refer to Figure <a href="#A3.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ C.3 Additional Results ‣ Appendix C Experiments and Results ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> and Figure <a href="#A3.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ C.3 Additional Results ‣ Appendix C Experiments and Results ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> in the Appendix <a href="#A3.SS3" title="C.3 Additional Results ‣ Appendix C Experiments and Results ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.3</span></a>, respectively.
Appendix <a href="#A3.SS3" title="C.3 Additional Results ‣ Appendix C Experiments and Results ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.3</span></a> (Tables <a href="#A3.T29" title="Table 29 ‣ C.3 Additional Results ‣ Appendix C Experiments and Results ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">29</span></a>–<a href="#A3.T31" title="Table 31 ‣ C.3 Additional Results ‣ Appendix C Experiments and Results ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">31</span></a>) presents a comprehensive comparison of knowledge editing methods across ZsRE, WikiData<math id="S5.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}_{\text{counterfact}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>counterfact</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\text{counterfact}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and larger LLMs such as Llama-2-13B and Qwen3-14B.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S5.T3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 3</span>: </span><span id="S5.T3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Performance comparison of knowledge editing methods applied to DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-8B on the ZsRE dataset, reporting results across four evaluation dimensions in both single and sequential editing settings, along with their average scores.</span></figcaption>
<div id="S5.T3.5" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:276.0pt;height:83.3pt;vertical-align:-40.4pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-104.8pt,31.6pt) scale(0.568315150898188,0.568315150898188) ;">
<table id="S5.T3.5.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S5.T3.5.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T3.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt" rowspan="2"><span id="S5.T3.5.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Method</span></td>
<td id="S5.T3.5.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt" colspan="5"><span id="S5.T3.5.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Single Editing</span></td>
<td id="S5.T3.5.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" colspan="5"><span id="S5.T3.5.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Sequential Editing</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T3.5.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T3.5.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_t"><span id="S5.T3.5.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span><span id="S5.T3.5.1.2.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><span id="S5.T3.5.1.2.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
<span id="S5.T3.5.1.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<span id="S5.T3.5.1.2.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S5.T3.5.1.2.1.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center">Rel.</span></span>
</span></span><span id="S5.T3.5.1.2.1.4" class="ltx_text"></span></td>
