<td id="S5.T3.5.1.12.11" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><span id="S5.T3.5.1.12.11.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">60.19</span></td></tr>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The experimental results reveal the following:
(1) Under autoregressive inference and evaluation based on semantic consistency, all parameter modification-based methods fall significantly short of the near-perfect single-edit performance reported in prior work.
(2) Most parameter modification-based methods, such as ROME and MEND, collapse under sequential editing scenarios: as the number of edits increases, all metrics quickly drop to near zero. This indicates a complete failure to retain knowledge across multiple updates.
(3) AlphaEdit maintains a stable performance rate across continuous edits, its Reliability and Generalization scores on LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct are only 69.49 and 55.50, respectively.
More importantly, it underperforms on Locality and Portability, with scores of just 8.38 and 8.58, highlighting its limited flexibility in applying edited knowledge.
It is worth noting that AlphaEdit demonstrates sensitivity to hyperparameters and model architecture. Further analysis can be found in Appendix <a href="#A3.SS6" title="C.6 AlphaEdit Hyperparameter Sensitivity Analysis ‣ Appendix C Experiments and Results ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.6</span></a>.
(4) All parameter-based editing methods are outperformed by SCR, a simple and intuitive retrieval-based reference baseline without modifying model parameters. This highlights the practical potential of external-knowledge-based approaches in real-world knowledge updating tasks. Rather than serving as a direct competitor to parameter-based editing methods, SCR provides a distinct empirical perspective by operating outside the parametric editing paradigm, facilitating a principled study of the efficiency–reliability trade-off.
Supplemental comparisons between SCR and parameter-based editing methods
are reported in Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Fairness of SCR vs. Parameter-Based Editing Methods ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a> (Tables <a href="#A4.T42" title="Table 42 ‣ Appendix D Fairness of SCR vs. Parameter-Based Editing Methods ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">42</span></a> and <a href="#A4.T43" title="Table 43 ‣ Appendix D Fairness of SCR vs. Parameter-Based Editing Methods ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">43</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">A fine-grained analysis over a wider range of edit counts (Table <a href="#A3.T37" title="Table 37 ‣ C.3 Additional Results ‣ Appendix C Experiments and Results ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">37</span></a> in Appendix <a href="#A3.SS3" title="C.3 Additional Results ‣ Appendix C Experiments and Results ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.3</span></a>) further confirms that while the timing of degradation varies by method, all parameter-based editing methods exhibit performance decline over longer edit sequences. Additionally,
there is consistent trends across both high- and low-confidence fact sets (Appendix <a href="#A3.SS3" title="C.3 Additional Results ‣ Appendix C Experiments and Results ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.3</span></a>, Table <a href="#A3.T38" title="Table 38 ‣ C.3 Additional Results ‣ Appendix C Experiments and Results ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">38</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ2: Can knowledge editing help reasoning LLMs integrate new facts without degrading reasoning ability?</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">As LLMs are increasingly applied to complex reasoning tasks, reasoning ability has become a key strength. Yet the effectiveness of knowledge editing methods for reasoning-oriented LLMs remains largely underexplored.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Tables <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 5.2 Experimental Results ‣ 5 Empirical Study ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and <a href="#A3.T32" title="Table 32 ‣ C.3 Additional Results ‣ Appendix C Experiments and Results ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">32</span></a> (Appendix <a href="#A3.SS3" title="C.3 Additional Results ‣ Appendix C Experiments and Results ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.3</span></a>) present results on ZsRE dataset and WikiData<math id="S5.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}_{\text{counterfact}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>counterfact</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\text{counterfact}}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.
To evaluate the retention of reasoning abilities after editing, we assess edited LLMs on a suite of mathematical reasoning benchmarks and broader downstream tasks (including GPQA-Diamond, ARC<math id="S5.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}_{\text{c}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>c</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\text{c}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and MMLU-Pro), reporting accuracy after 10 and 100 edits (Tables <a href="#A3.T33" title="Table 33 ‣ C.3 Additional Results ‣ Appendix C Experiments and Results ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">33</span></a> and  <a href="#A3.T34" title="Table 34 ‣ C.3 Additional Results ‣ Appendix C Experiments and Results ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">34</span></a> in Appendix <a href="#A3.SS3" title="C.3 Additional Results ‣ Appendix C Experiments and Results ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.3</span></a>).
The results consistently show that reasoning performance collapses after extensive sequential edits for most parameter-based editing methods, while AlphaEdit exhibits relative resilience.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Based on the comparison between Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 5.2 Experimental Results ‣ 5 Empirical Study ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and Table <a href="#A3.T29" title="Table 29 ‣ C.3 Additional Results ‣ Appendix C Experiments and Results ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">29</span></a>, knowledge editing methods that rely on parameter modification exhibit inferior performance on reasoning-oriented LLMs compared to general-purpose LLMs. For instance, AlphaEdit’s average performance in sequential editing scenarios drops from 35.48 to 24.16. This degradation may be due to the more implicit and distributed nature of knowledge representation in reasoning LLMs, as well as their greater dependence on long-range context reasoning. In contrast, SCR remains a strong practical reference baseline by integrating internal and external knowledge through selective context construction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Table <a href="#A3.T33" title="Table 33 ‣ C.3 Additional Results ‣ Appendix C Experiments and Results ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">33</span></a>
, ROME and RECT retain some performance, with average accuracy drops of 2.74 and 8.37, respectively. However, after 100 edits, their accuracy nearly drops to zero, indicating a complete collapse in reasoning ability. For MEND, accuracy remains at 0.00 after both 10 and 100 edits, suggesting that the method leads to total LLM failure. In contrast, AlphaEdit exhibits minimal performance degradation across all datasets, even after 100 sequential edits, and in some cases, exhibits slight improvements. Its parameter modifications can maintain reasoning accuracy close to that of the original LLM. Nevertheless, due to its limitations in locality and portability for knowledge editing, AlphaEdit may be better suited for general-purpose Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning rather than targeted knowledge editing tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p10.1" class="ltx_p">A detailed case analysis (see Appendix <a href="#A3.T39" title="Table 39 ‣ C.4 Failure Case Study on Editing Reasoning LLM ‣ Appendix C Experiments and Results ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">39</span></a>) reveals that editing reasoning-oriented LLMs presents unique challenges.
While editing methods can successfully guide the LLM to produce the correct next token, the edited LLM’s internal reasoning process often leads it to reflect outdated knowledge, thereby undermining the effectiveness of the edit. Moreover, in striving to preserve logical coherence within its reasoning trajectory, the edited LLM may generate explanations that are plausible-sounding but entirely fabricated. In some instances, the edited LLM may even disregard the original question in its attempt to round out the answer. These behaviors underscore key limitations of current knowledge editing methods when applied to reasoning-oriented LLMs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ3: Can knowledge editing methods generalize from factual knowledge to event knowledge?</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p12.1" class="ltx_p">While most existing research focuses on fact-based knowledge editing in the form of triples, real-world knowledge is often organized in a more complex manner, such as event.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p13.1" class="ltx_p">However, current studies on knowledge editing rarely evaluate methods on more complex, event-level datasets.
To address this limitation, we conduct knowledge editing experiments on Llama-2-7B-Chat, Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct, and DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-8B using the ELKEN dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib77" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peng et al., 2024</a>)</cite>,
the dataset processing code using GPT-4o is fully described in Appendix <a href="#A3.SS2" title="C.2 Dataset ‣ Appendix C Experiments and Results ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.2</span></a>.
Performance comparison between AlphaEdit and SCR on sequential editing tasks are shown in Figure <a href="#S5.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 5.2 Experimental Results ‣ 5 Empirical Study ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.
Detailed results for sigle editing and sequential editing are presented in Table <a href="#A3.T35" title="Table 35 ‣ C.3 Additional Results ‣ Appendix C Experiments and Results ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">35</span></a> and Table <a href="#A3.T36" title="Table 36 ‣ C.3 Additional Results ‣ Appendix C Experiments and Results ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">36</span></a>
(Appendix <a href="#A3.SS3" title="C.3 Additional Results ‣ Appendix C Experiments and Results ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.3</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p14.1" class="ltx_p">The results indicate that most parameter-based knowledge editing methods struggle to achieve satisfactory performance even in single-edit scenarios, and they almost completely break down under sequential editing. For instance, although AlphaEdit performs well on fact-based datasets, its performance deteriorates significantly when applied to event-level editing tasks involving multiple entities and attributes occurring concurrently. This degradation is primarily due to the limited ability of these methods to capture complex semantic relationships between entities and to integrate contextual information across interconnected elements via parameter-level adjustments. In contrast, SCR consistently achieves the best results across nearly all settings we evaluate. These findings underscore the practical limitations of parameter updates for editing small amounts of knowledge, while highlighting the substantial potential of in-context learning in LLMs for real-world applications.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p15" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p15.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ4: How do different knowledge editing methods compare in terms of time efficiency?</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p16.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond correctness and robustness, latency and efficiency are equally crucial for practical deployment. Effective editing methods should minimize both editing overhead and inference latency.
To systematically evaluate these aspects, we consider two key metrics: (i) <span id="S5.SS2.p16.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">edit time</span>, the wall-clock time required to apply an individual edit, and (ii) <span id="S5.SS2.p16.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">inference time</span>, defined as the average wall-clock latency per input query normalized by the base LLM’s latency, measured under greedy decoding with a fixed output length of 50 tokens post-edit.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F4" class="ltx_figure">
<p id="S5.F4.3" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center ltx_align_center"><span id="S5.F4.3.1" class="ltx_text"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2606.00570v1/fig_elken_alphaedit_vs_scr_comparison.svg" id="S5.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/200;" width="476" height="200"></object></span></p>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>: </span><span id="S5.F4.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
Performance comparison between AlphaEdit and SCR on sequential editing tasks using the ELKEN dataset.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS2.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p17.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S5.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 5.2 Experimental Results ‣ 5 Empirical Study ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> illustrates the trade-off between editing time and inference latency for parameter-based and external knowledge–based methods, with detailed results for all methods reported in Figure <a href="#A3.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ C.3 Additional Results ‣ Appendix C Experiments and Results ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> in the Appendix <a href="#A3.SS3" title="C.3 Additional Results ‣ Appendix C Experiments and Results ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.3</span></a>. Regarding editing time, methods that integrate knowledge through progressively more abstract representations, from textual edits to embeddings, hidden states, and ultimately parameter updates, exhibit increasing editing costs. This trend reflects the growing computational overhead associated with deeper and more indirect knowledge integration. In contrast, parameter-based editing methods do not introduce additional inference latency, as they directly modify the original model parameters.
External knowledge-based methods, such as SCR, generally incur higher inference costs, primarily because of retrieval operations or the need to handle extended input sequences.
Overall, no method achieves strong performance on both axes simultaneously. This reveals a fundamental limitation of current model editing paradigms: intrusive parameter updates trade update efficiency for inference efficiency, while non-intrusive methods preserve fast updates at the expense of increased inference latency.
A more detailed analysis of SCR’s VRAM consumption, inference time, and performance stability across memory sizes and top-k settings is provided in Appendix <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Efficiency-Performance Analysis of SCR ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a> (Tables <a href="#A5.T44" title="Table 44 ‣ Appendix E Efficiency-Performance Analysis of SCR ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">44</span></a>–<a href="#A5.T46" title="Table 46 ‣ Appendix E Efficiency-Performance Analysis of SCR ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">46</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F5" class="ltx_figure">
<p id="S5.F5.3" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center ltx_align_center"><span id="S5.F5.3.1" class="ltx_text"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2606.00570v1/fig_tradeoff_pareto.svg" id="S5.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:381/209;" width="381" height="209"></object></span></p>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 5</span>: </span><span id="S5.F5.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
Trade-off between editing time and inference latency relative to the base model across different editing paradigms.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This paper shows that the limitations of parameter-based knowledge editing are rooted in the geometry of LLM representation spaces. Building on dimensional collapse, we develop a theoretical framework that models editing as a first-order perturbation of hidden representations and proves that bounded weight updates can induce disproportionately large distortions along low-variance principal directions, with such effects compounding under sequential editing. This identifies a concrete mechanism by which localized parameter modifications lead to global failures in reasoning and generalization.
Extensive experiments under realistic settings corroborate these theoretical predictions: across varying knowledge complexity, numbers of edits, and evaluation dimensions, parameter-based editing methods consistently degrade core LLM capabilities, with performance deteriorating as edits accumulate, largely independent of the specific editing algorithm.
Taken together, our results reveal a fundamental stability–efficiency trade-off in knowledge updating: methods that enable efficient, localized modifications can compromise the global stability of model representations, while mechanisms that better preserve representational integrity are often less efficient. Understanding, formalizing, and managing this trade-off is therefore a central challenge for future knowledge update methods.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Acknowledgements</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This work was supported by the National Natural Science Foundation of China (Grant No. 72204087), the Chenguang Program of Shanghai Education Development Foundation and Shanghai Municipal Education Commission (Grant No. 23CGA28), the Shanghai Pujiang Program (Grant No. 23PJC030), and the Young Elite Scientists Sponsorship Program by CAST (Grant No. YESS20240562). We thank the ECNU LINK Lab for providing essential computational resources and a supportive research environment, which were instrumental in carrying out this study. We are also deeply grateful to the anonymous reviewers for their thoughtful comments and suggestions, which greatly improved the quality of this paper. Finally, we acknowledge the inspiring contributions of prior works in the field, as well as the open-source methods and datasets utilized in our study, which laid a critical foundation for this research.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Impact Statement</h2>

<div id="Sx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This paper establishes a rigorous, practice-oriented evaluation framework for knowledge editing methods,
grounded in a geometric framework that explains why parameter-based knowledge editing methods may destabilize LLM representations.
By revealing that even carefully targeted weight modifications systematically degrade core model capabilities, especially under realistic sequential editing conditions,
our work encourages the community to shift focus from isolated factual accuracy metrics to adopting holistic evaluation standards that account for reliability, generalization, locality, portability, and reasoning preservation.
We believe this shift in evaluation practice can steer future research toward more robust and trustworthy knowledge updating paradigms.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">At the same time, we acknowledge two limitations of our analysis. First, our theoretical framework relies on specific assumptions (dimensional collapse, small perturbations, and local representation stability) and should be regarded as a falsifiable hypothesis rather than a conclusive theory. Second, although our findings highlight consistent shortcomings of current parameter-based editing methods, they should not be considered a wholesale rejection of parameter-based editing methods, which may still prove effective under carefully constrained conditions or with improved regularization. We hope this work inspires principled progress rather than deterring further exploration.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, as with any technique enabling targeted modification of LLM behavior, advanced knowledge editing tools present inherent dual-use risks. However, the evaluation standards and diagnostic tools proposed in this work can help detect and mitigate potential misuse.</p>
</div>
</section>
