<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Justifications for Working Hypotheses and Empirical Support</h2>

<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.1 </span>Hypothesis <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Assumption 4.1 (Dimensional Collapse Hypothesis). ‣ 4.1 Working Hypotheses and Problem Formulation ‣ 4 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>: Dimensional Collapse</h3>

<div id="A2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This subsection evaluates whether the hidden representations in our studied models exhibit the spectral characteristics required by Hypothesis <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Assumption 4.1 (Dimensional Collapse Hypothesis). ‣ 4.1 Working Hypotheses and Problem Formulation ‣ 4 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Literature Motivation</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Prior research on the geometry of representations in neural networks has consistently documented phenomena such as anisotropy, rank collapse, and low-dimensional concentration in Transformer hidden states. These findings suggest that hidden representation matrices can be effectively modeled as having a compressed singular value spectrum.
Recent empirical studies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib88" title="" class="ltx_ref">Valeriani et al., 2023</a>)</cite> specifically indicate that hidden representations in intermediate layers of large Transformer models experience a significant contraction in intrinsic dimensionality. This contraction implies that the representations lie near a low-dimensional manifold with a small effective rank and a rapidly decaying singular value spectrum, supporting the assumption of a near-zero minimum singular value (<math id="A2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\text{min}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mtext>min</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\text{min}}</annotation></semantics></math>) in our hypothesis.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Further supporting evidence comes from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib79" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pinter &amp; Elhadad (2023)</a></cite>, who observe that editing operations in large language models (LLMs) cause a pervasive ”ripple effect” in the hidden representation space. This suggests structural fragility, which aligns with the geometric characteristics of dimensional collapse. Additionally, mechanistic analyses <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib103" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xie et al., 2025</a>)</cite> link specific editing failures to low-variance substructures in attention modules, highlighting the vulnerability of critical information encoded in directions with low variance and sensitivity to perturbation.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hidden State Extraction</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">For each evaluated model (Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct, DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-8B, Llama-2-13B), we sample <math id="A2.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=100000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=100000</annotation></semantics></math> input prompts from the C4-en dataset.
Hidden states <math id="A2.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> are then extracted using forward hooks implemented in PyTorch during forward passes, capturing layer-specific hidden representations before and after each editing operation. Specifically, hidden states are collected from the down-projection layer of each Transformer block’s feed-forward network. For the T5-3B encoder-decoder model, hidden representations are captured at the input and output projections of feed-forward networks in the layers targeted by MEND (i.e., encoder layers 22–23, decoder layers 22–23), following the default hyperparameter settings for MEND.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Singular Value Spectrum Analysis</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">For each layer, we construct the hidden representation matrix <math id="A2.SS1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A2.SS1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> represents the feature dimension and <math id="A2.SS1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=100000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=100000</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of samples.
A full singular value decomposition (SVD) <math id="A2.SS1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H=U\Sigma V^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H=U\Sigma V^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> is performed using PyTorch’s GPU-accelerated torch.linalg.svd implementation. This yields the ordered singular value spectrum <math id="A2.SS1.p8.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{\sigma_{1}\geq\sigma_{2}\geq\dots\geq\sigma_{d}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\sigma_{1}\geq\sigma_{2}\geq\dots\geq\sigma_{d}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, which provides insights into the representation space’s intrinsic dimensionality and capacity.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS1.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Empirical Validation</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">We empirically analyze the singular value spectra of hidden representations across models and layers:</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="A2.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A2.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ B.1 Hypothesis : Dimensional Collapse ‣ Appendix B Justifications for Working Hypotheses and Empirical Support ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> reports dimensional collapse statistics for Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A2.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ B.1 Hypothesis : Dimensional Collapse ‣ Appendix B Justifications for Working Hypotheses and Empirical Support ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> reports the same analysis for DeepSeek-R1-Distill-LLaMA-8B.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A2.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ B.1 Hypothesis : Dimensional Collapse ‣ Appendix B Justifications for Working Hypotheses and Empirical Support ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> reports the same analysis for LlaMA-2-13B.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A2.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ B.1 Hypothesis : Dimensional Collapse ‣ Appendix B Justifications for Working Hypotheses and Empirical Support ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> reports the same analysis for selected encoder and decoder projections in T5-3B.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p12.1" class="ltx_p">Across all models, architectures, and configurations, we consistently observe the following trends: (1) low effective rank, (2) vanishingly small singular values in the spectral tail, and (3) large condition numbers. Although the degree of collapse varies by layer and architecture, these results strongly align with the hypothesis of dimensional collapse. Consequently, they provide empirical support for interpreting dimensional collapse as the spectral precondition underlying the relative amplification mechanisms explored in Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Relative Perturbation Amplification in Collapsed Directions ‣ 4 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
