</section>
<section id="A2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.2 </span>Hypothesis <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem2" title="Assumption 4.2 (Small Perturbation Hypothesis). ‣ 4.1 Working Hypotheses and Problem Formulation ‣ 4 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>: Small-Perturbation Regime</h3>

<div id="A2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This subsection examines whether single-edit perturbations remain sufficiently small to render the first-order approximation in Eq. (<a href="#S4.E8" title="Equation 8 ‣ 4.2 Relative Perturbation Amplification in Collapsed Directions ‣ 4 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>) an informative analytical framework.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Literature Motivation</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Empirical research on the unintended side effects of editing models highlights a strong connection between the magnitude of weight updates and the degradation of model performance. Large weight updates, characterized by high Frobenius norms <math id="A2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\Delta W\|_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\Delta W\|_{F}</annotation></semantics></math>, have been shown to induce overfitting and diminish generalization capabilities <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gu et al., 2024a</a>)</cite>. In contrast, editing techniques such as RECT, which explicitly constrain the magnitude of weight changes, have demonstrated effectiveness in mitigating these adverse effects <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gu et al., 2024a</a>)</cite>. This body of evidence underscores the importance of small, localized parameter perturbations for successful editing. Further validation comes from failure analyses, which link catastrophic model collapse directly to the generation of excessively large parameter updates <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib110" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2024e</a>)</cite>.
Collectively, these findings justify the adoption of a first-order Taylor expansion as a locally linear approximation to analyze the effects of small edits.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Perturbation Norm Measurement</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate five editing methods (AlphaEdit, MEMIT, MEND, ROME, WISE) by applying a single edit to each model and measuring the resulting hidden state changes across layers. Specifically, we compute the hidden-state perturbation <math id="A2.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta h=h_{\text{edit}}-h_{\text{orig}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mtext>edit</mtext></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mtext>orig</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta h=h_{\text{edit}}-h_{\text{orig}}</annotation></semantics></math> for each edited layer and record its <math id="A2.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> norm <math id="A2.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\Delta h\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\Delta h\|</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="A2.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=100000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=100000</annotation></semantics></math> input samples. To test Assumption <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem2" title="Assumption 4.2 (Small Perturbation Hypothesis). ‣ 4.1 Working Hypotheses and Problem Formulation ‣ 4 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>, which posits that single-edit perturbations remain in the small regime where a first-order Taylor approximation is appropriate, we report the mean, standard deviation, minimum, and maximum of these norms.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Empirical Validation</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">We quantify the norm of hidden-state perturbations induced by single edits across a variety of methods and models:</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="A2.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A2.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A2.T9" title="Table 9 ‣ B.2 Hypothesis : Small-Perturbation Regime ‣ Appendix B Justifications for Working Hypotheses and Empirical Support ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> reports single-edit perturbation norms in Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A2.T10" title="Table 10 ‣ B.2 Hypothesis : Small-Perturbation Regime ‣ Appendix B Justifications for Working Hypotheses and Empirical Support ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> reports the same analysis for DeepSeek-R1-Distill-LLaMA-8B.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A2.T11" title="Table 11 ‣ B.2 Hypothesis : Small-Perturbation Regime ‣ Appendix B Justifications for Working Hypotheses and Empirical Support ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> reports the same analysis for LlaMA-2-13B.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A2.T12" title="Table 12 ‣ B.2 Hypothesis : Small-Perturbation Regime ‣ Appendix B Justifications for Working Hypotheses and Empirical Support ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a> reports the same analysis for T5-3B under MEND.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">In decoder-only models, perturbation norms are generally small in most edited layers, supporting the validity of local first-order analyses for single edits. However, the results for T5 reveal a greater degree of layerwise heterogeneity, suggesting that the small-perturbation regime is influenced by both architectural and layer-specific factors. These findings support the use of Eq. (<a href="#S4.E8" title="Equation 8 ‣ 4.2 Relative Perturbation Amplification in Collapsed Directions ‣ 4 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>) as a local analytical approximation for understanding single-edit effects, while also clarifying its limitations in more heterogeneous settings.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.T9" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="A2.T9.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 9</span>: </span><span id="A2.T9.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Perturbation norms induced by a single edit in selected layers of Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct.</span></figcaption>
