<section id="A2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.3 </span>Hypothesis <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem3" title="Assumption 4.3 (Local Representation Stability Hypothesis). ‣ 4.1 Working Hypotheses and Problem Formulation ‣ 4 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>: Local Representation Stability</h3>

<div id="A2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This subsection investigates whether principal directions in the representation space remain approximately stable under sequential editing.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Literature Motivation</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Recent studies have shown that model editing can substantially distort the global geometry of hidden representations, a phenomenon termed ”representation shattering,” which is strongly correlated with performance degradation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nishi et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. These findings suggest that maintaining the structural stability of the representation space is essential for preserving model functionality after editing.
Furthermore, widespread observations of unintended geometric disruptions, such as the hidden space ripple effect <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib90" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, reinforce concerns about the challenges of achieving stable, localized updates. This aligns with broader skeptical perspectives on the feasibility of treating large language models (LLMs) as reliably editable knowledge repositories <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib79" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pinter &amp; Elhadad, 2023</a>)</cite>.
We therefore adopt the approximate stability of principal component directions <math id="A2.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> as a working hypothesis to describe a stable editing regime.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Principal Component Stability Analysis</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In the context of sequential editing (<math id="A2.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=1,2,\ldots,all" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=1,2,\ldots,all</annotation></semantics></math> edits), we extract the principal component matrices <math id="A2.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{\text{edit}}^{(T)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mtext>edit</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{\text{edit}}^{(T)}</annotation></semantics></math> from the hidden states after each <math id="A2.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-th edit and compare them to the original principal component matrix <math id="A2.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{\text{orig}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mtext>orig</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{\text{orig}}</annotation></semantics></math> of the unedited model. To assess the geometric consistency between the top-<math id="A2.SS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> principal components of <math id="A2.SS3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{\text{edit}}^{(T)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mtext>edit</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{\text{edit}}^{(T)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{\text{orig}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mtext>orig</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{\text{orig}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we calculate the minimum principal angle <math id="A2.SS3.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{\text{min}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>min</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{\text{min}}</annotation></semantics></math> between their respective subspaces. To further quantify alignment, we compute the cosine of the minimum angle, <math id="A2.SS3.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cos\theta_{\text{min}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>min</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cos\theta_{\text{min}}</annotation></semantics></math>. A value of <math id="A2.SS3.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cos\theta_{\text{min}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>min</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cos\theta_{\text{min}}</annotation></semantics></math> close to 1 indicates strong alignment and stability, while values approaching 0 suggest near-orthogonality and instability. This analysis is performed across a range of subspace dimensions <math id="A2.SS3.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\{10,50,100,200,500,1000,2000\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>50</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>100</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>200</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>500</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1000</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2000</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\{10,50,100,200,500,1000,2000\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Empirical Validation</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate the cosine similarity between the original principal directions and those obtained after sequential edits to quantify the stability of principal component directions under sequential editing. We perform this analysis for both decoder-only and encoder-decoder models. For decoder-only models, including Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct, DeepSeek-R1-Distill-LLaMA-8B, and LlaMA-2-13B, we conduct experiments with AlphaEdit and ROME. For the encoder-decoder model T5-3B, we examine the FFN input-projection (<math id="A2.SS3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>) and output-projection (<math id="A2.SS3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{o}</annotation></semantics></math>) spaces under edits performed with MEND. The results are summarized in the following tables:</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="A2.I3" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A2.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Tables <a href="#A2.T13" title="Table 13 ‣ B.3 Hypothesis : Local Representation Stability ‣ Appendix B Justifications for Working Hypotheses and Empirical Support ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>, <a href="#A2.T14" title="Table 14 ‣ B.3 Hypothesis : Local Representation Stability ‣ Appendix B Justifications for Working Hypotheses and Empirical Support ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>, and <a href="#A2.T15" title="Table 15 ‣ B.3 Hypothesis : Local Representation Stability ‣ Appendix B Justifications for Working Hypotheses and Empirical Support ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a> report the cosine similarity between the original and edited principal directions at layer 30 of Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct, DeepSeek-R1-Distill-LLaMA-8B, and LLaMA-2-13B, respectively, under sequential edits with AlphaEdit.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Tables <a href="#A2.T16" title="Table 16 ‣ B.3 Hypothesis : Local Representation Stability ‣ Appendix B Justifications for Working Hypotheses and Empirical Support ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>, <a href="#A2.T17" title="Table 17 ‣ B.3 Hypothesis : Local Representation Stability ‣ Appendix B Justifications for Working Hypotheses and Empirical Support ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>, and <a href="#A2.T18" title="Table 18 ‣ B.3 Hypothesis : Local Representation Stability ‣ Appendix B Justifications for Working Hypotheses and Empirical Support ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a> report the corresponding results for the same models under sequential edits with ROME.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Tables <a href="#A2.T19" title="Table 19 ‣ B.3 Hypothesis : Local Representation Stability ‣ Appendix B Justifications for Working Hypotheses and Empirical Support ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a> and <a href="#A2.T20" title="Table 20 ‣ B.3 Hypothesis : Local Representation Stability ‣ Appendix B Justifications for Working Hypotheses and Empirical Support ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a> report the cosine similarity between the original and edited principal directions in the FFN input-projection and output-projection spaces of T5-3B under sequential edits with MEND.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Across models, dominant PCs often remain relatively stable during the initial edits, while lower-variance PCs deteriorate rapidly as edits accumulate. In T5-3B, certain layers exhibit a loss of stability almost immediately after editing begins. These observations reinforce the interpretation of Hypothesis <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem3" title="Assumption 4.3 (Local Representation Stability Hypothesis). ‣ 4.1 Working Hypotheses and Problem Formulation ‣ 4 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a> as a short-horizon idealization and underscore that the practical degradation of representation geometry may exceed the predictions of this simplified scenario.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.T13" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="A2.T13.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 13</span>: </span><span id="A2.T13.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Stability of principal component directions at layer 30 of Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct under sequential editing using AlphaEdit.
