<section id="A2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.4 </span>Generalization Across Model Scales and Architectures</h3>

<div id="A2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The main text focuses on 7B–14B decoder-only LLMs, but the geometric signatures underlying our analysis extend beyond a single model. The dimensional-collapse statistics (Tables <a href="#A2.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ B.1 Hypothesis : Dimensional Collapse ‣ Appendix B Justifications for Working Hypotheses and Empirical Support ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>–<a href="#A2.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ B.1 Hypothesis : Dimensional Collapse ‣ Appendix B Justifications for Working Hypotheses and Empirical Support ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>), perturbation norms (Tables <a href="#A2.T9" title="Table 9 ‣ B.2 Hypothesis : Small-Perturbation Regime ‣ Appendix B Justifications for Working Hypotheses and Empirical Support ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>–<a href="#A2.T12" title="Table 12 ‣ B.2 Hypothesis : Small-Perturbation Regime ‣ Appendix B Justifications for Working Hypotheses and Empirical Support ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>), PC-stability analyses (Tables <a href="#A2.T13" title="Table 13 ‣ B.3 Hypothesis : Local Representation Stability ‣ Appendix B Justifications for Working Hypotheses and Empirical Support ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>–<a href="#A2.T20" title="Table 20 ‣ B.3 Hypothesis : Local Representation Stability ‣ Appendix B Justifications for Working Hypotheses and Empirical Support ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a>), and <math id="A2.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> distributions (Tables <a href="#A2.T21" title="Table 21 ‣ B.5.2 Layerwise distributions of relative change rates ‣ B.5 Direct Empirical Validation of the Amplification Mechanism ‣ Appendix B Justifications for Working Hypotheses and Empirical Support ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">21</span></a>–<a href="#A2.T24" title="Table 24 ‣ B.5.2 Layerwise distributions of relative change rates ‣ B.5 Direct Empirical Validation of the Amplification Mechanism ‣ Appendix B Justifications for Working Hypotheses and Empirical Support ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">24</span></a>) consistently reveal evidence of the same vulnerability mechanism across DeepSeek-R1-Distill-LLaMA-8B, LlaMA-2-13B, and specific components of T5-3B.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, the encoder-decoder architecture exhibits greater heterogeneity, with certain layers departing from the small-perturbation regime more rapidly. As a result, we do not assert identical quantitative patterns across all architectures. Instead, we posit that the amplification mechanism arising from dimensional collapse is broadly applicable. The severity of this mechanism depends on factors including architecture type, layer characteristics, editing method, and editing horizon.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.5 </span>Direct Empirical Validation of the Amplification Mechanism</h3>

<div id="A2.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This subsection evaluates the core prediction from Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Relative Perturbation Amplification in Collapsed Directions ‣ 4 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>: perturbation amplification is strongest in low-singular-value directions and can be significant even after a single edit.</p>
</div>
<section id="A2.SS5.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">B.5.1 </span>Directional amplification along collapsed directions</h4>

<div id="A2.SS5.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS5.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS5.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Relative Change Rate <math id="A2.SS5.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> Computation</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS5.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS5.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For each editing method and each edited layer, we calculate the directional relative change rate <math id="A2.SS5.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> as:</p>
<table id="A2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{k}=\frac{\sqrt{n}\,|c_{k}|}{\sigma_{k}},\quad c_{k}=u_{k}^{T}\Delta h," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{k}=\frac{\sqrt{n}\,|c_{k}|}{\sigma_{k}},\quad c_{k}=u_{k}^{T}\Delta h,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS5.SSS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.SS5.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS5.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> are derived from the SVD of the original hidden representation matrix <math id="A2.SS5.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\text{orig}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mtext>orig</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\text{orig}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A2.SS5.SSS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta h=h_{\text{edit}}-h_{\text{orig}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mtext>edit</mtext></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mtext>orig</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta h=h_{\text{edit}}-h_{\text{orig}}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the per-sample perturbation. For each layer, we compute <math id="A2.SS5.SSS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> across all <math id="A2.SS5.SSS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> principal directions, summarizing the mean, median, maximum, standard deviation, and the proportion of directions where <math id="A2.SS5.SSS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{k}&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{k}&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS5.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS5.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS5.SSS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Visualization and Key Insights</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS5.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS5.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In Figure <a href="#A2.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ B.5.1 Directional amplification along collapsed directions ‣ B.5 Direct Empirical Validation of the Amplification Mechanism ‣ Appendix B Justifications for Working Hypotheses and Empirical Support ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, we visualize the empirical distributions of the directional relative change rate <math id="A2.SS5.SSS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> across different editing methods, language model architectures, and editing steps. The x-axis represents the singular value index <math id="A2.SS5.SSS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, while the y-axis (logarithmic scale) denotes <math id="A2.SS5.SSS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, reflecting the relative perturbation amplification. A critical threshold at <math id="A2.SS5.SSS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{k}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{k}=1</annotation></semantics></math> (dashed line) separates directions with tolerable perturbations (<math id="A2.SS5.SSS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{k}&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{k}&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>) from those exhibiting severe distortion (<math id="A2.SS5.SSS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{k}&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{k}&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS5.SSS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS5.SSS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Several key patterns emerge from this analysis:</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS5.SSS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="A2.I4" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A2.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I4.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Cumulative Perturbation Amplification:</span> Across all methods and models, <math id="A2.I4.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> consistently increases with higher editing steps, confirming the cumulative nature of perturbation amplification.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I4.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sensitivity in Low-Singular-Value Directions:</span> Directions corresponding to smaller singular values are disproportionately affected. Even after a single edit, many of these directions exhibit <math id="A2.I4.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{k}\gg 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>≫</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{k}\gg 1</annotation></semantics></math>, in alignment with the theoretical predictions in Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 4.5 (Relative Amplification in Low-Scale Directions). ‣ 4.2 Relative Perturbation Amplification in Collapsed Directions ‣ 4 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I4.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I4.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I4.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I4.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Method-Specific Differences:</span> Editing methods vary in their impact on <math id="A2.I4.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. For example, AlphaEdit demonstrates greater stability over sequential edits, whereas methods like MEMIT and ROME exhibit sharp amplification, particularly during later editing steps.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I4.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I4.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I4.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I4.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Model-Specific Sensitivity:</span> Architecture differences also influence sensitivity to perturbation. For instance, models like DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-8B exhibit extreme <math id="A2.I4.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> values in low-singular-value directions after multiple editing iterations, suggesting heightened vulnerability to editing-induced distortions in these collapsed dimensions.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS5.SSS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS5.SSS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">These findings provide direct empirical validation of the theoretical amplification mechanism outlined in Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Relative Perturbation Amplification in Collapsed Directions ‣ 4 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>, particularly in low-singular-value dimensions.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.F6" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2606.00570v1/R_k_5method_3model.png" id="A2.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:429/574;" width="429" height="574" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F6.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 6</span>: </span><span id="A2.F6.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Directional relative change rate <math id="A2.F6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> across editing methods, model architectures, and steps.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS5.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">B.5.2 </span>Layerwise distributions of relative change rates</h4>

<div id="A2.SS5.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS5.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We also report the layerwise distributions of <math id="A2.SS5.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> for single edits across various language models and editing methods:</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS5.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="A2.I5" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A2.I5.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I5.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I5.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A2.T21" title="Table 21 ‣ B.5.2 Layerwise distributions of relative change rates ‣ B.5 Direct Empirical Validation of the Amplification Mechanism ‣ Appendix B Justifications for Working Hypotheses and Empirical Support ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">21</span></a> reports detailed statistics of <math id="A2.I5.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> distributions for Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I5.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I5.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I5.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A2.T22" title="Table 22 ‣ B.5.2 Layerwise distributions of relative change rates ‣ B.5 Direct Empirical Validation of the Amplification Mechanism ‣ Appendix B Justifications for Working Hypotheses and Empirical Support ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">22</span></a> reports the same analysis for DeepSeek-R1-Distill-LLaMA-8B</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I5.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I5.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I5.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A2.T23" title="Table 23 ‣ B.5.2 Layerwise distributions of relative change rates ‣ B.5 Direct Empirical Validation of the Amplification Mechanism ‣ Appendix B Justifications for Working Hypotheses and Empirical Support ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a> reports the same analysis for LlaMA-2-13B</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I5.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I5.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I5.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A2.T24" title="Table 24 ‣ B.5.2 Layerwise distributions of relative change rates ‣ B.5 Direct Empirical Validation of the Amplification Mechanism ‣ Appendix B Justifications for Working Hypotheses and Empirical Support ‣ Revisiting Parameter-Based Knowledge Editing in Large Language Models: Theoretical Limits and Empirical Evidence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">24</span></a> reports the same analysis for T5-3B</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS5.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS5.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">These tables show that even a single edit often yields <math id="A2.SS5.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{k}&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{k}&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> in a large fraction of directions and can produce extremely large mean and maximum values.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.T21" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="A2.T21.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 21</span>: </span><span id="A2.T21.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Layer-wise distribution of relative change rates (<math id="A2.T21.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>) for a single edit on Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct.</span></figcaption>
<table id="A2.T21.5" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="A2.T21.5.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A2.T21.5.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt"><span id="A2.T21.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Method</span></td>
<td id="A2.T21.5.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><span id="A2.T21.5.1.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Layer</span></td>
<td id="A2.T21.5.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_tt"><math id="A2.T21.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">R</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{k}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.T21.5.1.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> mean</span></td>
<td id="A2.T21.5.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_tt"><math id="A2.T21.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">R</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{k}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.T21.5.1.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> min</span></td>
<td id="A2.T21.5.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_tt"><math id="A2.T21.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">R</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{k}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.T21.5.1.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> max</span></td>
<td id="A2.T21.5.1.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_tt"><span id="A2.T21.5.1.6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Std</span></td>
<td id="A2.T21.5.1.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><span id="A2.T21.5.1.7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">num(<math id="A2.T21.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{k}&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{k}&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>)</span></td>
<td id="A2.T21.5.1.8" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><span id="A2.T21.5.1.8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">%(<math id="A2.T21.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{k}&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{k}&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T21.5.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A2.T21.5.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t"><span id="A2.T21.5.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">AlphaEdit</span></td>
<td id="A2.T21.5.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span id="A2.T21.5.2.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">5</span></td>
<td id="A2.T21.5.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_t"><math id="A2.T21.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.39\times 10^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">3.39</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn mathsize="0.900em">10</mn><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.39\times 10^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A2.T21.5.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_t"><math id="A2.T21.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7.43\times 10^{-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">7.43</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn mathsize="0.900em">10</mn><mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7.43\times 10^{-2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A2.T21.5.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_t"><math id="A2.T21.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.38\times 10^{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">1.38</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn mathsize="0.900em">10</mn><mn mathsize="0.900em">6</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.38\times 10^{6}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
