<section id="A2.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.6 </span>Functional Relevance: Correlation with Editing Performance</h3>

<div id="A2.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This subsection examines whether perturbation amplification is functionally associated with practical editing degradation.
Rather than treating amplification at the individual layer level, we evaluate whether <em id="A2.SS6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">step-level</em> summaries of the layer-wise <math id="A2.SS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> distribution correlate with step-level editing performance metrics.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step-level <math id="A2.SS6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> Summaries.</span>
For each editing method and edit step, we first summarize the layer-wise <math id="A2.SS6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> distribution using several scalar features, including the median, upper quantiles (q90 and q95), and trimmed mean.
Because the editing metrics are defined at the step level rather than the layer level, we then aggregate these layer-wise summaries across edited layers to obtain a single step-level amplification statistic.
To test the robustness of the observed trends, we consider three cross-layer aggregation strategies: mean, max, and median.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS6.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS6.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Correlation Protocol.</span>
We measure the association between step-level statistics derived from <math id="A2.SS6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and editing performance using Spearman’s rank correlation coefficient.
We use Spearman correlation because the number of valid edit steps is limited for several methods and the relationship between amplification and performance degradation is not necessarily linear.
We exclude MEND from this analysis because its performance under sequential 